# Copy to openspec/config.yaml (merge into it if one already exists).
#
# `context:` is injected into EVERY artifact prompt; `rules:` only into the
# matching artifact. Keep context short — it is always loaded, so it earns
# harder pruning than anything else in this bundle.

schema: mattpocock-bridge

context: |
  Read ./TOOLS.md before starting work in this repo, and again before changing
  dependencies, scripts, build config, test setup, or deploy settings. It is the
  canonical map of this project's stack, commands, and available agent capabilities.
  Take the package manager and command names from it rather than the ones you would
  reach for by default. Where the repo has no TOOLS.md, the mapping-project-tooling
  skill builds one from evidence on disk.

  Before exploring, read CONTEXT.md (or CONTEXT-MAP.md, then each relevant
  CONTEXT.md) and any ADR under docs/adr/ touching the area in play. Where these
  files are absent, proceed silently — domain-modeling creates them when a term
  is actually resolved. Name domain concepts with the terms CONTEXT.md defines
  rather than synonyms it avoids. Where output contradicts an ADR, say so and
  make the case for reopening it.

  Six stores outlive a change, so write each fact to the one that owns it: behaviour
  goes to openspec/specs/ as requirements, vocabulary to CONTEXT.md as a glossary and
  nothing else, decisions that still bind the next change to docs/adr/, product truth
  to PRODUCT.md, visual tokens to DESIGN.md, and stack, commands, and agent
  capabilities to TOOLS.md. Anything written inside openspec/changes/<name>/ archives
  with the change and is read afterwards as history.

  Put closed questions — a fork with two to four named answers — to the user through
  AskUserQuestion, keeping the recommendation and the ruled-out alternatives in the
  prose above it: the tool carries the choice, the prose carries the reasoning. Fall
  back to a numbered prose question where AskUserQuestion is unavailable.

  Issue tracker configuration is at docs/agents/issue-tracker.md.

  On UI work this project uses impeccable and not Anthropic's frontend-design skill.
  Two design skills with different vocabularies cancel each other out, so leave
  frontend-design disabled.

# Artifact ids are shared across schemas: `specs` and `tasks` exist in both
# mattpocock-bridge and bugfix-flow, so rules keyed to them fire in both. Keys that
# exist in only one schema (`surface`, `design`, `diagnose`) fire only there.

rules:
  diagnose:
    - "Confirm the seam for the regression test with the user before marking this artifact done."
  surface:
    - "Name the visitor mode from the surface itself, not from what the product sells."
  design:
    - "Confirm the Seams table with the user before marking this artifact done."
    - "Check TOOLS.md for a local wrapper or an already-installed library covering this capability before proposing a new dependency."
  tasks:
    - "Apply the ready-for-agent label to every published slice."
    - "A slice changing a command, script, dependency, package manager, runtime version, directory convention, or deploy target updates TOOLS.md in the same slice and refreshes its Last verified date. Documentation deferred to a later slice is documentation that does not land."

operations:
  apply:
    guidance:
      - "Take commands from TOOLS.md rather than from habit; the package manager it records is the one the lockfile resolved."
      - "Run the affected test files as you go; run the full suite once the slice is done."
      - "Where the slice changed TOOLS.md facts, run the mapping-project-tooling drift check before committing."
      - "On a UI slice, run the impeccable detector before review and fix what it reports."
